// EXPECT: FAIL
// ERROR_CONTAINS: ANUBIS_LOOP_INVARIANT_UNVERIFIABLE
// SOUNDNESS: a `break` in EXPRESSION position (`let sink = break;` — parsed Expr::Call{callee:"break"})
// escapes the loop, but the loop-invariant escape check `stmt_escapes_loop` only recognised statement-form
// break/continue and a bare `return`. So the engine verified the invariant `x <= 10` inductively (base +
// preservation), assumed `x <= 10 && !(x < 10)` ⇒ `x == 10` after the loop, and statically DISCHARGED
// `assert(x == 10)` — but the `break` exits on the first iteration with `x == 0`, so the runtime assert
// panics (or, wrapped in an `ensures`, is never re-checked at all). Now `expr_escapes_loop` detects the
// embedded break and the invariant is (correctly) unverifiable, so the check fails closed.
fn main() {
    let mut x = 0;
    while x < 10 invariant(x <= 10) {
        let sink = break;
        x = x + 1;
    }
    assert(x == 10);
}
